[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Jobava London System"]
[Variation "Against 3...e6"]
[ECO "D00"]
[Character "sharp"]
[Risk "medium"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "fork"]
[Themes "c7-pressure"]

1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 { Starts the Jobava set-up: the knight comes out before the c-pawn moves, so it can jump to b5 later. } 2... Nf6
3. Bf4 { The bishop gets out before e3 would shut it in, and it already looks at c7. [%cal Gf4c7] } 3... e6
4. Nb5 { Threatens Nc7+, forking king and rook. Black's queen alone guards c7, and the bishop on f4 also hits that square. [%cal Gb5c7,Gf4c7] }
4... Bb4+ ( 4... Na6 { Guards c7, but the knight is badly placed on the edge. } 5. e3 c6 6. Nc3 { Its work is done, so the knight goes back
and the bishop on f1 is free to develop. } 6... Bb4 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Ne2 c5 9. a3 )
5. c3 { Blocks the check, and the pawn on c3 prepares b4 against a bishop on a5. } 5... Ba5 ( 5... Be7? { The bishop leaves the pin on c3 but gives
up control of c7. } 6. Nxc7+ { The knight forks king and rook. Taking it with the queen loses her to Bxc7, because c7 is hit twice. [%csl Rc7] } 6... Kf8 7. Nxa8 )
6. b4 { Chases the bishop away from the a5-e1 diagonal. } 6... Bb6
7. a4 { Threatens a5, which would trap the bishop: its retreat squares are covered by the knight and the bishop. [%cal Ga4a5] } 7... a6?!
{ Attacks the knight, but White does not need to retreat it. } 8. a5 { The pawn attacks the bishop first, so the pieces trade off. } 8... axb5
9. axb6 { Takes the bishop; Black's rook takes on a1 and the queen recaptures. } 9... Rxa1 10. Qxa1 { The queen recaptures on the open a-file. } 10... Na6
11. bxc7 { The pawn attacks the queen and cannot be taken: the c7 square is still covered by the bishop. } 11... Qe7
12. e3 { Finally opens the bishop on f1. White keeps the strong pawn on c7. } *
